you're watching the daily climate show  coming up the chancellor announces a 25  tax on oil and gas profits but what does  this mean for investment in green energy  scotland plans to ban sales of the most  damaging single-use plastic items  and as monkey pox cases rise a warning  there could be more diseases spreading  from animals to humans without major  steps to protect wildlife and their  natural habitats  [Music]  hello and welcome to the daily climate  show where we track the changes  happening to our planet hear from the  leaders tasked with solving the problems  and explore solutions  now millions of people will welcome the  chancellor's announcement of a windfall  tax on the profits of oil and gas  companies and plans to use this levy to  help counter the rising cost of living  but some analysts are worried that rishi  sunak's plans will damage the investment  needed to build better energy security  and the uk's net zero ambitions so let's  hear what the chancellor had to say  companies will have a new and  significant incentive to reinvest their  profits  the new levy will be charged on profits  of oil and gas companies at a rate of 25  it will be temporary and when oil and  gas prices return to historically more  normal levels the levy will be phased  out with a sunset clause written into  the legislation  and crucially and crucially  with our new investment allowance  we are nearly doubling the overall  investment relief for oil and gas  companies  that means for every pound a company  invests  they'll get back 90  in tax relief  so the more a company invests the less  tax they will pay  well our climate change correspondent  hannah thomas peter joins us now from  westminster so hannah what impact will  this new step have on the fight against  climate change  i think amongst uh climate change  campaigners and various think tanks  there's broad satisfaction that  unearned unexpected profits of the oil  and gas industry are now being subject  to this windfall tax  there is also some consternation that if  you look closely at the detail actually  what the chancellor is doing here is  addressing a cost of living crisis  rather than the climate crisis  directly because the policy contains  real incentives to invest in uk  extraction not renewables and in their  view for campaigners this rather misses  an opportunity to kick-start  or accelerate even more investment in  the the the green energy transition and  also perhaps even misses opportunities  to invest in things like an insulation  program and so there there's real worry  about that that this was a missed  opportunity there was also separate  consternation about the chancellor  mentioning that he was considering  appropriate steps to further tax other  electricity generators who are enjoying  massive profits and that includes of  course not just the nuclear industry but  wind and solar too  i mean what are the oil and gas  companies saying about it  well they are predictably unhappy  shell has just released a statement  saying  we have consistently emphasized the  importance of a stable environment for  long-term investment and then has gone  on to outline plans to invest in a  low-carbon future and its plans to be a  part of the energy transition the not  very subtle inference there being if you  take money away from us all of that is  less is less assured but the industry  lobby group the offshore energies uk  lobby group has gone much further  calling the windfall tax a disappointing  and worrying development for a sector  that supports 200 000 uk jobs calling it  a backwards step that makes the uk seem  like an unpredictable place to do  business  but the bit that they've been really  pointed about is saying that there is  going to be a real impact on oil and gas  companies plans for investing in clean  energy mainly wind and a little bit of  solar and the the offshore energies uk  group saying that is actually  fundamentally going to make it much much  harder for the uk to hit its net zero  targets as a result  hannah thanks very much indeed  let's take a look now at some of the  day's other climate news and the sale of  plastic straws cutlery polystyrene cups  and food boxes will be banned in  scotland from the 1st of june the ban is  part of measures to clamp down on the  most environmentally damaging single-use  plastic items while reducing waste and  pollution the ban also covers all  single-use polystyrene food containers  and their lids as well as plastic  stirrers balloon sticks plates and  coffee stirrers  work has begun to turn  000 hectares of land in england into  nature recovery projects there are a  total of five projects across the peak  district the west midlands  cambridgeshire norfolk and somerset and  all aim to help tackle wildlife loss and  improve access to nature the projects  will share 2.4 million pounds in funding  from defra and natural england as part  of the government's target to halt the  decline in species abundance by 2030 and  protect 30 of english land by 2030.  [Applause]  a committee of mps is warning that  emissions must be reduced in the  construction of buildings if the uk is  to meet its net zero ambitions  their report states that 25 percent of  the country's total greenhouse gas  emissions can be attributed to the built  environment  but there is little government guidance  on how to reduce emissions from the  manufacture of materials through to  construction maintenance of buildings  and eventual demolition  [Applause]  now the number of monkey pox infections  around the world is continuing to rise  both wales and northern ireland have  confirmed their first cases today and  experts are warning we could see more  zoonotic diseases those transmitted from  animals to humans without better  regulation of the wildlife trade and  better care of natural habitats now more  than 250 cases of the virus have been  confirmed globally in the last three  weeks the uk has recorded the largest  number with 79 cases while spain has  confirmed 59 and portugal 39 cases  well joining us now to discuss this is  dr nigel seizer who's the executive  director of the research group  preventing pandemics at source and tell  us a little bit more about zoonotic  diseases these that transfer from  animals to humans are they on the rise  first of all  yes if you look over the last 50 years  we've seen a highly significant increase  in the number of new diseases  moving from wild wildlife into  humans diseases like sars nepa  ebola zika covid19 is one of these most  likely and and monkeybox which was first  detected about 40 years ago about 300  new diseases have been found in humans  in recent decades  so why is that happening what's the main  driver of this this rise in this kind of  transmission  so about 60 of these new diseases come  from wildlife they're what we call  zoonotic diseases and and this is  following from ever increasing  intensification of agriculture clearing  of forests especially in the tropical  regions uh increasing commercial  exploitation of wildlife more and more  people impinging on the natural world  towns and cities in tropical forest  areas  the use of wildlife  the exotic animal trade and  intensification of animal agriculture  farming of cattle pigs and poultry has  also contributed to this risk so how do  we go about stopping it  well the key thing is to greatly  increase efforts to protect tropical  forests to to regulate strictly enforce  close down some of the wildlife trade  and commercial use of wildlife including  things like some of the exotic pet trade  coming into europe and the united states  as well as what people might have seen  more in the news which is the live  animal markets in places like china  parts of africa and latin america and so  on all these things contribute to risk  and another really key area is doing a  much better job to address how we treat  and take care of of livestock pigs and  poultry in particular  and very briefly you mentioned covert  there has that helped focus minds do you  think that could help prompt change the  fact that the world has suffered this  pandemic  well we've been working intensively on  this throughout the kovit 19 pandemic  and i have to say we're actually very  disappointed to see that most  governments are not giving much more  attention to the to the actual origins  of these diseases i mean the good news  is that the things we need to do  to address climate change are also some  of the things that would reduce the risk  of future pandemics like better  protecting tropical forests other things  as well like taking better care of  wildlife addressing risks in the live  animal trade and improving the  regulation and controls around  intensification of animal agriculture  these are all things that we should be  doing anyway and covid19 and now  monkeypox to some extent as well remind  us that much more effort is needed in  these areas  dr nigel sizer very interesting to talk  to you thanks very much indeed  thank you  well that's it for me thank you for  watching see you next time  [Music]  you

